Z||000004||Ozzy||06-23-2000||02:33 AM||||Homocidal...<P>There are a myriad of audio/stereo adapters available at Radio Shack that would work. 3mm stereo to RCA, RCA to RCA split etc, etc. The only thing I can think of might be loading up too much stuff on your audio card. Most of the devices you want to drive should be high impedance though. Ask your friendly Radio Shark dealer. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000005||Homicidal||06-23-2000||02:36 AM||||Nah, it's all output!  Nothing is going INTO the card.  I might lose some sound quality though.....||||reg||1
Z||000006||Ozzy||06-23-2000||02:54 AM||||I understand...even though it is an output, it is possible to load the output transistors of the sound card excessively causing possible damage. I would try it and if there is any distortion disconnect it and try another solution.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000007||phillip||06-23-2000||10:53 AM||||might look kind of junky but this might be easiest. get it all at radio shack.<P>a mini stereo headphone 2 into 1 jack, the type that let 2 people listen to one walkman, and a mini stereo plug to 2 rca patch cable. that should do it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Shadow Wolf||06-25-2000||03:44 AM||||Depending on your sound hardware, I'd suggest plugging it into the line out jack.  If you DON'T have a direct line out jack on your soundcard (or motherboard, beit the case these days) there is often a jumped which can be set to alter the speaker out into a line out.||||reg||1
